11.1 Conditional branching
The if statement executes a block of code only when a condition (a logical value) is TRUE. The block is delimited by curly braces { }:

x = 4

if (x &gt; 0) {
  sqrt(x)
}

[1] 2


If the condition is FALSE, the block is simply skipped and nothing happens. An optional else block runs instead when the condition is FALSE:

x = -4

if (x &gt; 0) {
  sqrt(x)
} else {
  print("x is negative: cannot take the square root")
}

[1] "x is negative: cannot take the square root"


You can chain several conditions with else if. This is useful to classify values into categories, for example size classes of a tree:

dbh = 35

if (dbh &lt; 10) {
  size_class = "seedling"
} else if (dbh &lt; 30) {
  size_class = "sapling"
} else {
  size_class = "adult"
}
size_class

[1] "adult"


Only the first condition that is TRUE is executed, so the order matters.
Note that the condition of an if statement must be a single logical value. If you want to test many values at once (a vector), use ifelse (several ones can be nested):

dbh = c(35, 9, 22)
ifelse(dbh &lt; 10,  # First argument is condition
       "seedling", # Second is the value returned if true
       ifelse(dbh &lt; 30, # Third is the value returned if false (can be nested ifelse)
              "sapling",
              "adult"))

[1] "adult"    "seedling" "sapling"

